 {
  "id": "L12/p1",
  "lesson": "L12",
  "kind": "prediction",
  "prompt": "<p>You train two identical models with identical compute: one on a raw crawl, one on the same crawl after removing duplicates and junk, which leaves 40% of the text. Which reaches the lower loss on clean held-out text?</p>",
  "answer": "b",
  "answerKind": "choice",
  "tol": 0,
  "options": [
   {
    "key": "a",
    "html": "<p>The raw crawl, because it has 2.5 times as much data.</p>"
   },
   {
    "key": "b",
    "html": "<p>The cleaned crawl, because every token it trains on is unique and useful.</p>"
   },
   {
    "key": "c",
    "html": "<p>They are equal, because compute is equal.</p>"
   },
   {
    "key": "d",
    "html": "<p>It depends only on the model size.</p>"
   }
  ],
  "explain": "<p>At fixed compute both models see the same number of tokens. On the raw crawl many of those tokens are repeats or junk, so the model effectively trains on less, and on text unlike the held-out set. Deduplication and filtering usually help at equal compute: Lee et al. (2022) found that models trained on deduplicated data matched or beat the originals in fewer steps and emitted memorized training text far less often. The capstone measures the effect on your own corpus.</p>"
 },
 {
