 {
  "id": "L12/p3",
  "lesson": "L12",
  "kind": "prediction",
  "prompt": "<p>A quality classifier is trained to separate Wikipedia articles (label 1) from random crawl pages (label 0), and you keep pages scoring above 0.5. Which good text is most at risk of being removed?</p>",
  "answer": "c",
  "answerKind": "choice",
  "tol": 0,
  "options": [
   {
    "key": "a",
    "html": "<p>Well-edited encyclopedic text.</p>"
   },
   {
    "key": "b",
    "html": "<p>Spam.</p>"
   },
   {
    "key": "c",
    "html": "<p>Good writing in styles absent from Wikipedia, such as dialogue, poetry or informal forums.</p>"
   },
   {
    "key": "d",
    "html": "<p>Nothing: a classifier with high accuracy only removes junk.</p>"
   }
  ],
  "explain": "<p>The classifier learns what the reference looks like, not what quality is. Text far from the reference in style or dialect scores low however good it is, so aggressive classifier filtering narrows the distribution. Combine classifiers with rules, keep documents stochastically rather than by a hard cutoff, and check what was removed.</p>"
 },
 {
  "id": "L12/p4",
  "lesson": "L12",
  "kind": "prediction",
  "prompt": "<p>A 4-gram model and a bigram model are trained on a corpus that, unknown to you, contains the evaluation documents. The text comes from a process where only the previous word matters. Which looks better on the evaluation set?</p>",
  "answer": "a",
  "answerKind": "choice",
  "tol": 0,
  "options": [
   {
    "key": "a",
    "html": "<p>The 4-gram model, by a wide margin.</p>"
   },
   {
    "key": "b",
    "html": "<p>The bigram model, because it matches the process.</p>"
   },
   {
    "key": "c",
    "html": "<p>They look the same.</p>"
   },
   {
    "key": "d",
    "html": "<p>Neither: the leak makes both scores infinite.</p>"
   }
  ],
  "explain": "<p>The 4-gram model memorizes the exact sequences it saw, including the leaked evaluation text, so its evaluation loss collapses. On fresh text the bigram model is better. Contamination does not just inflate a score; it can pick the wrong model. Exercise 11 measures both.</p>"
 },
 {
  "id": "L12/concept1",
  "lesson": "L12",
  "kind": "concept",
  "prompt": "Why does MinHash estimate Jaccard similarity?",
  "answer": "For a random hash, the minimum over the union of two sets is equally likely to be any element, and the two minima agree exactly when it lies in the intersection, which has probability |A and B| / |A or B|.",
  "answerKind": "text"
 },
 {
  "id": "L12/concept2",
  "lesson": "L12",
  "kind": "concept",
  "prompt": "What does banding a MinHash signature achieve?",
  "answer": "Two documents become candidates if any band of r rows matches, with probability 1 - (1 - s^r)^b: an S-curve, so only likely near-duplicates are compared.",
  "answerKind": "text"
 },
 {
  "id": "L12/concept3",
  "lesson": "L12",
  "kind": "concept",
  "prompt": "What is train-test contamination, and how is it detected?",
  "answer": "Evaluation text present in the training data; it is found by n-gram overlap, such as shared 13-word sequences, and removed or reported separately.",
  "answerKind": "text"
 },
 {
  "id": "L12/concept4",
  "lesson": "L12",
  "kind": "concept",
  "prompt": "How is a data loader made exactly resumable?",
  "answer": "Derive every shuffle from the seed and epoch so the state is a position, and save any stateful part, such as a shuffle buffer and its generator, in the checkpoint.",
  "answerKind": "text"
 },
 {
  "id": "L12/concept5",
  "lesson": "L12",
  "kind": "concept",
  "prompt": "How many epochs does a domain get under mixture weights?",
  "answer": "A domain sampled with weight p in a run of T tokens is repeated p T / size times.",
  "answerKind": "text"
 },
 {
  "id": "L12/concept6",
  "lesson": "L12",
  "kind": "concept",
  "prompt": "What is the risk of filtering with a quality classifier?",
  "answer": "It learns what its reference text looks like, so it can remove good text in other styles or dialects and narrow the training distribution.",
  "answerKind": "text"
 },
